## What this licence does NOT cover

Source time series are the property of their publishers and are governed by
their own terms. They are NOT redistributed in this repository. Values quoted
in the documentation (dated readings in docs/backtest.md, for example) are
cited with attribution and remain their publishers' data.

The outputs you generate by running the code do contain values of some source
series. Those values stay under their publishers' terms, not under CC-BY:

  - cfnai_ma3 (three-month average of CFNAI), nfci
      Federal Reserve Bank of Chicago, retrieved from FRED
  - pce_trimmed_12m (PCETRIM12M159SFRBDAL)
      Federal Reserve Bank of Dallas, retrieved from FRED
  - t5yifr, t10y2y
      Federal Reserve Bank of St. Louis, retrieved from FRED

    These five columns carry the FRED status "Copyrighted: Citation
    required": commercial use and display are allowed with attribution to
    the originating source and to FRED; sub-licensing is not.
    https://fred.stlouisfed.org/legal/

  - ciss (output/regime_current.json only)
      European Central Bank, ECB Data Portal: free use provided the ECB is
      cited as the source, the data are reproduced accurately, and any
      modification is stated (here, a calendar-month average of daily values).
  - sahmrealtime, fedfunds, and the DTWEXBGS, MCOILBRENTEU and DFII10 values
    in regime_current.json
      Public domain, retrieved from FRED (citation requested).

The standalone series written by the run (cfnai-national-activity-index,
trimmed-mean-pce-inflation, euro-area-ciss-systemic-stress) are source series
under their publishers' terms; each .xlsx carries them in a "Source" sheet.

Never written to any output:

  - ICE BofA indices (e.g. BAMLH0A0HYM2) — ICE Data Indices, LLC
  - VIX (VIXCLS) — Cboe Global Markets
  - OECD composite leading indicators (USALOLITOAASTSAM, G7LOLITOAASTSAM) — OECD

The SOS-style indicator (sos) is computed by Eco3min from the insured
unemployment rate (IURSA, U.S. Department of Labor, public domain) with the
method of O'Trakoun and Scavette (Economics Letters, 2025). It is not the
Federal Reserve Bank of Richmond series.

